{
 "set": {
  "evidenceSummary": "Practitioner-focused narrative review that proposes a Braking Performance Framework for horizontal deceleration in multi-directional sports, built on the neuromuscular and biomechanical determinants of deceleration. It sorts training methods into three categories: braking elementary exercises (for example high eccentric loading, including flywheel work, and eccentric yielding isometrics), braking development exercises (for example fast eccentric loading and overcoming isometrics) and braking performance exercises (unanticipated, contextual and game-specific decelerations). The authors give programming tables and note limited research on assisted braking steps and unknown dose-response for several methods.",
  "paper.findings": "The authors propose a framework with three exercise categories: braking elementary exercises that build eccentric and tissue capacity, braking development exercises that target fast eccentric and concentric force and rate of force development, and braking performance exercises that train deceleration under game-like perceptual demands. They give programming guidance, such as in-season flywheel scheduling, and note limited research on heavy assisted braking steps and an unknown minimum effective dose for supplementary deceleration training.",
  "paper.methods": "Narrative review and practical framework, with no described literature search. The authors draw on the known determinants of horizontal deceleration and on published studies of eccentric, isometric, flywheel, plyometric and change-of-direction training to propose exercise categories and programming tables.",
  "paper.practicalImplications": "Practitioners can use the framework to choose and progress braking exercises, moving from elementary eccentric and isometric work through development exercises toward unanticipated and game-specific decelerations. Progressions should be checked against the athlete's response, since dose-response evidence is limited."
 },
 "verification": {
  "status": "CORRECTED",
  "changes": [
   "evidenceSummary/findings: framework described as two categories with overcoming isometrics and change of direction under braking performance -> three categories, with overcoming isometrics under braking development ('three main exercise categories: 1) braking elementary exercises, 2) braking development exercises, and 3) braking performance exercises')",
   "evidenceSummary: 'eccentric quasi-isometric' work is not in the text -> removed; elementary methods listed as printed ('high eccentric loading ... eccentric yielding-isometrics')",
   "paper.findings: dose-response limitation made specific ('the minimum effective dosage is currently unknown')"
  ],
  "residualConcerns": "Volume and issue appear only inside the DOI (v4i1), not printed as citation details, so they were omitted. Year 2024 is printed in the citation header.",
  "spotCheckPriority": "high"
 }
}

