{
 "set": {
  "evidenceSummary": "Retrospective cohort of 39 primary hamstring strain injuries in 30 adult male professional players at one English club over 2010 to 2019, where each injury had an MRI within 7 days of onset. Two musculoskeletal radiologists graded the MRIs with the British Athletics Muscle Injury Classification (BAMIC) and with Peetron's system, and the grades were compared with return to play (mean 16.5 days). BAMIC grade correlated weakly with return to play (r = 0.32, 95% CI 0.01 to 0.58, p = 0.05), oedema length correlated weakly and not significantly (r = 0.3, p = 0.06), cross-sectional area did not correlate (r = 0.02), and the two grading systems agreed only slightly (kappa = 0.21)."
 },
 "verification": {
  "status": "CORRECTED",
  "changes": [
   "evidenceSummary: added that the 39 injuries came from 30 players and the mean return to play ('39 HSI from 30 professional football players'; 'overall mean for RTP was 16.5 (SD 12.2 ...) days')"
  ],
  "residualConcerns": "Return-to-play topic. Text is the journal pre-proof; year 2022 taken from the 'Please cite' line, and no volume or pages are printed. Several players contributed more than one injury, which the correlations do not account for.",
  "spotCheckPriority": "high"
 }
}

